WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:02.840
We present the Video
Mesh, a new data

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:05.390
structure for video editing.

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:08.000
The Video Mesh encodes
the motion, layering,

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.210
and 3D structure of a video
sequence in a unified data

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:12.590
structure.

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:16.309
Intuitively, it looks like a
paper cut out of the world.

00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:20.750
We illustrate our technique on
a simple educational example.

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:23.030
Constructing a video
mesh begins with first

00:00:23.030 --> 00:00:26.540
tracking distinctive features
through the sequence.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:30.420
Then we compute a Delaney
triangulation on each frame.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:32.630
The mesh linearly
interpolates the motion

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:35.120
at vertices, which is a
good approximation where

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:37.310
the motion is smooth.

00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:39.320
Users specify
occlusion boundaries

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:42.170
at a few key frames using
splines, which separates

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:44.630
the components in the mesh.

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:46.800
Splines are advected
between key frames

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.765
by the optical flow
encoded in the mesh.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:54.210
Now we change the
viewpoint to reveal

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:56.700
the geometry of the mesh.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.175
The user specifies the
depth at a few vertices.

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:12.330
Now the video mesh looks
like a 2.5D paper cut out.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:14.730
Notice how the actor's
hand occludes his body

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:17.250
while remaining connected
to it through the arm.

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:20.010
Such self-occlusion cannot
be represented by traditional

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:22.950
layers without arbitrarily
cutting the arm.

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:25.050
In contrast, the
Video Mesh naturally

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:28.170
encodes these topological
configurations.

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:31.790
We use off the shelf matting
to decompose the texture.

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:33.990
We inpaint the missing
parts of the scene

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:36.210
using texture synthesis.

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:38.010
Once we have the
Video Mesh, we can

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:41.970
use classical computer graphics
techniques for special effects.

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:44.730
For instance, we simulate
a large aperture lens

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:47.220
with a shallow depth of
field by rendering the scene

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:51.480
several times from viewpoints
sampled over the aperture.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:55.640
The user clicks to choose
the plain in focus.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:59.180
We can also composite a
simulation of volumetric smoke.

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:02.210
Note how the attenuation depends
on the depth of the scene.

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:08.919
This sequence features
a static camera

00:02:08.919 --> 00:02:10.900
with complex occlusions
between the columns.

00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:22.480
The user needs to specify
only the primary occlusion

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:24.070
boundaries.

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:26.410
Since the camera is
static, we interactively

00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:30.530
assign depth using simple
image based modeling tools.

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:33.560
We insert a ground plane and
model the columns and the actor

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:34.535
as vertical facades.

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:43.150
Since the actor is a
single connected component

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:46.180
in the Video Mesh, we can
copy him in space and time

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:48.310
to turn him into a crowd.

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:51.100
Since we have 3D geometry,
we can interactively

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:52.330
manipulate the actor.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:07.970
We can also refocus the
camera, move the camera,

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:11.090
and simulate the vertigo effect
by moving the camera backward

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:13.790
while zooming in so that
the main object does not

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:16.010
change sides.

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:18.620
Since we have the 3D
geometry of the scene,

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:20.570
we can adjust the
lighting, as well as

00:03:20.570 --> 00:03:22.400
simulate a fog
effect that accounts

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:24.820
for the depth at each pixel.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:26.800
This allows us to
render this sequence

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:28.070
as if it were shot at night.

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:34.120
This sequence features
a moving camera

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:37.870
in a scene with multiple
rigidly moving objects.

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:40.030
We use off the shelf
structure for motion

00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:44.540
to recover 3D vertex
positions and the camera path.

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:46.310
In this case,
structure for motion

00:03:46.310 --> 00:03:49.850
was applied independently on the
chassis and lid of the copier

00:03:49.850 --> 00:03:53.090
and registered into the
same coordinate system.

00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:55.830
We composite multiple
copies of another video mesh

00:03:55.830 --> 00:03:57.840
into the scene
while interactively

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.030
changing the viewpoint.

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:03.740
Notice the parallax induced
by the changing viewpoint.

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:05.415
The inset shows the input video.

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:12.540
This video of a riverbank
near the Notre Dame Cathedral

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:17.250
features complex geometry and
occlusions between buildings.

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:22.360
We model the 3D geometry using
the same image-based tools.

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:24.730
We cut the mesh at the
primary occlusion boundaries

00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:27.460
with splines, insert
two ground plains,

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:31.240
and model the buildings
with vertical facades.

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:31.990
The Video Mesh.

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:34.330
Lets us move the camera
onto the roof of the boat

00:04:34.330 --> 00:04:36.750
as it sails down the river.

